Subject: The Best and Worst of IBM's AIX?
Message-ID: <1992Nov8.183139.12122@netcom.com>
Sender: taylor@netcom.com (Dave Taylor)
Organization: SunWorld Magazine, San Francisco, CA: +1 (415) 267-1728
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 1992 18:31:39 GMT
Lines: 52

Just like other Unix implementations, AIX differs in a variety of
ways from the 'standard' (whatever that is nowadays).  I would like
to hear from some AIX folk on the following questions, and would
be most appreciative of any feedback resultant:

+ How does AIX differ from SVR4?  SunOS?  HP-UX?  BSD4.3?

+ What has IBM added that makes AIX *better* than other Unixes?

+ What has IBM added that makes AIX *worse* than other Unixes?

+ Is source from the net easily ported onto the AIX platform?  And are
  programs written for the AIX system portable?

+ What about X, and Motif?  Are they standard and compatible?  What
  version of X11 is with the existing version of AIX?

+ What do you think of "smit"?  What about the rest of the system
  administration tools?  

+ If you could change one thing in the AIX OS, what would it be and why?

+ If there was one thing in AIX that you'd like to migrate to the rest
  of the Unix industry, what would it be?

+ IBM touts the SAA connectivity and ability for the AIX systems to
  plug right into existing IBM networks, mainframe and otherwise.
  Have you found that true?  

+ What about the RS/6000 series?  What are your thoughts about it,
  how did you come to the decision to purchase it, and how do you
  think it compares to other workstations from Sun, HP, DEC, etc?

+ If you had it all to do again, would you buy another IBM RISC
  workstation with AIX?

+ Finally, whatever happened to NeXTstep on AIX, and what do you
  think about it, PowerPC, and perhaps the possibility of Windows
  NT on the RS platform?

THANKS GREATLY for your assistance on this: if you're interested in
a summary of the answers to this question, just note as such in your
message and I'll arrange for you to get a copy via email.

PLEASE RESPOND VIA EMAIL ONLY!
					Thanks.

						-- Dave Taylor
SunWorld Magazine
San Francisco, California

