In response to the question Andrew M. raises about OS's and SVID/ OSF-AES
spec's, I think that it depends on how you look at systems.  From an
application programmer's point of view, the interfaces he/she deals
with are probably the most important.  For that, specifying something like
XPG compliance (current is XPG4 but it's only been out for a short while.
Almost everyone is tested against XPG3 up to now.  If you get XPG-compliance
you'll get OSF/AES and SVID along with it, as well as POSIX.  From that
standpoint, I don't know if the brand of OS is important.

From a system administrator's standpoint, differences can be quite
interesting among OS's.  Sun is trying to get to Solaris 2.1, which 
is SVR4 (mostly), with everyone.  I'd certainly be wary of that
transition and make sure my application vendors had already moved
before I did.  Note that if you want to play with Sun's latest toys,
you WILL be running Solaris 2.1.

HP/UX is a semistrange blend of SVR3.2 on top of a Berkeley file system.
It does work pretty well.  Be careful here of things you need being
unbundled.  Also note that HP doesn't seem to have network printing
figured out terribly well.  Sun can print to Newsprint(ers) but not to
printers on terminal servers, if that's important.  HP, being rather
system V like, has odd notions about remote printing.  However, HP
can probably give you a program that will more or less end printouts
to an IP address but it's not supported and has some interesting flaws
like not releasing printer ports.

AIX is about as strange a UNIX as I've ever worked on.  Again, it is
stable and stays up well.  It's got the logical volume manager that
OSF is using for their file systems.  Where IBM is headed is toward
a Mach kernel that will have AIX/OSF and OS/2 "personalities".  That's
a year off or so.

DEC is clearly going to OSF/1.  They are in the midst of their transition
now.

If you want a summary, how about this:

	Sun	moving to Solaris 2.1 which is SVR4 - BIG change

	HP	sitting mostly on the fence with their own SVR3 and
		BSD blend

	IBM	moving, though slowly, to a Mach kernel

	DEC	moving to OSF/1

I personally am not sure I'm finding stability out there with any of them.
I think you've got to decide when you want pain and go from there.

Tony


